vo_avfoundation: redraw only the canvas tiles a frame changes

Every frame of subtitles that differed from the last was cleared and
blended whole into its overlay buffer. Measured on an iPhone Air (2736x1260
canvas, counters and a pixel comparison of every canvas with the one before
it): a typeset frame clears 0.8-1.5 Mpx and walks 0.9-1.7 Mpx of bitmap
rectangles, while 0.016-0.063 Mpx of it differ from the previous canvas,
inside 3-5 % of its 32 px tiles. The per-buffer dirty rects of 0043 bound
the clear by the rectangles last drawn, which are mostly empty and get
merged: 4.3x the covered pixels were cleared, 13.7 % of the process.

The canvas is cut into 32 px tiles. A tile's signature folds in, in drawing
order, every part that reaches into it: the id of its pixels (common 0234;
without one the item's change id and the part's place in it), its place,
size and color. Each pool buffer remembers the signatures of what it holds
(the buffers are recycled with their contents), and a frame drawn into it
leaves alone every tile whose signature repeats, copies from the previous
canvas the tiles that one already holds, and clears and blends the rest,
each part clipped to them. Tile rows that need the same treatment are done
as one band. Every pixel of a redrawn tile gets the same blends in the same
order as before, so the buffer ends up bit for bit as a full clear and a
full blend leave it. A frame with a part that needs scaling (CoreGraphics)
is still drawn whole. avf_dirty_set and its test are gone; the composite
modes go through the same path.

scripts/test_avf_osd_damage.sh drives the marked region verbatim against a
model pool (rotations of 1-6 buffers, evicted entries, moving, changing and
static parts, cut tiles): every buffer equals a full redraw. Clipping moves
pixels between the NEON kernels and their scalar tails, so
test_avf_osd_blend.sh now also checks the BGRA-over-BGRA kernel against its
scalar reference for every alpha, channel and destination value.

A buffer whose contents are unknown (new to the pool, as every buffer of
plain dialogue is: the pool frees them after a second) is cleared whole in
one piece and is blank from there. One 13.8 MB memset runs about three
times as fast per byte as the same canvas cleared band by band around the
subtitles (0.6 ms against 1.1-1.4 ms a canvas), which first cost real plain
dialogue 2.5 % (3.82 -> 3.92 % of a core, n = 6).

iPhone Air, vo=avfoundation, interleaved with the unpatched build, 30 s
windows, median (range), no dropped frame in any run:
  anime-ass.mkv (synthetic typesetting)  14.75 (14.72-14.80) -> 9.55
    (9.48-9.57) % of a core, CPU energy 8.70 -> 5.03 mJ/s (n = 3)
  a real opening (komi11, 72 bitmaps)    13.88 (13.75-13.90) -> 12.54
    (12.51-12.58) %, 7.84 -> 6.97 mJ/s (n = 3; libass and the atlas are
    the rest)
  real plain dialogue                    3.82 (3.79-3.97) -> 3.82 (3.81-3.95) %
  synthetic plain dialogue               3.40 (3.37-3.44) -> 3.39 (3.34-3.42) %
    (n = 6 each)
  no subtitles                           3.25 -> 3.24 %
Footprints equal. With a build that compares every canvas in process with
a full clear and blend of the same items, 20 runs (five fixtures, both
apps' option sets, with and without 0049's option; each pauses, seeks,
plays into the end, reloads, switches the subtitles off and on and has its
layer rotated, resized and set back) differ in 0 pixels of about 4400
canvases; paused screenshots against the unpatched build are identical in
12 of 12 pairs, plain, after a resize and after a rotation and back.

